The Statistical Horizon: A Scaling Benchmark and Procedural
Caution for Institutional Use of AI Text Watermarking
Henri F. Van Ryn
Independent Researcher
henri.vanryn@gmail.com
February 10, 2026 (revised June 23, 2026)
Version 5.8 — Preprint
Abstract
AI text watermarking has evolved from academic speculation into industrial practice. Yet
watermark detections remain probabilistic indicators rather than proof of authorship. This paper
formalizes the Statistical Horizon as a structural scaling benchmark for finite-sample watermark
detectability. In the stylized mean-shift model used here, required sample length scales inversely
with the square of the combined signal term ηδ, creating a steep, inverse-quadratic penalty for
low-visibility watermarking. The paper argues that this scaling result should not be confused
with a rule for strong institutional attribution. Under the model developed here, the Statistical
Horizon nh is best understood as a lower detectability floor: it corresponds to the point at
which expected detector signal reaches threshold and, in the Gaussian power analysis, to a
50% detection-power point. Stronger institutional use generally requires longer text than this
floor alone would suggest. To make that gap explicit, the paper also defines a higher-power
reliability benchmark nE, showing that practical institutional interpretation depends not only
on watermark strength but also on desired power, calibration, sample length, robustness, and
disclosure. The resulting framework is intended to discipline how watermark signals are relied
upon rather than to weaken watermarking technology as such. Keywords: AI watermarking;
statistical inference; forensic science; institutional reliance; machine learning governance; digital
provenance; AI policy.
1 Introduction
1.1 Motivation and the Interpretive Gap
The rapid proliferation of Large Language Models (LLMs) has forced a parallel evolution in digital
provenance. As synthetic text becomes increasingly difficult to distinguish from human writing at
the semantic level, watermarking has emerged as a leading technical strategy for attribution. These
approaches—ranging from synonymous substitution to logit “green-list” biasing—attempt to embed
a recoverable statistical signal in generated text. Yet the interpretive problem begins where the
engineering result ends. Technical watermarking literature evaluates systems in terms of detectability:
z-scores, likelihood ratios, power, and related performance measures. Institutional and adjudicative
use, however, asks a different question: what, if anything, a detector output warrants as evidence.
Those questions are related, but they are not identical. A detector can be capable of signaling under
some conditions without thereby yielding robust evidence of authorship in disciplinary, regulatory,
or forensic settings. Watermark detection is not the discovery of a hidden cryptographic tag. It
1
is a probabilistic inference drawn from a finite text sample under model-dependent assumptions.
This paper formalizes the Statistical Horizon: a structural scaling benchmark that marks the
sample-length regime in which expected detector signal reaches a specified threshold under stated
assumptions. The goal is not to undermine watermarking technology, but to discipline its evidentiary
use. Watermark evidence should therefore be treated as an indicative and assumption-sensitive
forensic signal, not as an oracle of authorship. The paper’s central claim is narrow but consequential.
The horizon equation identifies a structural relationship between watermark strength, detector
response, and sample length. It does not by itself yield a universal admissibility threshold, nor does
it license a fixed token rule across watermarking systems. On the contrary, the framework shows
that required text length is highly sensitive to hidden or undisclosed parameters. In low-visibility
regimes, the sample lengths needed for practically informative detection may be much larger than
casual policy discussion assumes. This matters because short-text detector outputs are especially
vulnerable to overinterpretation. In settings where watermark strength, calibration data, and system
parameters are not disclosed, external reviewers cannot reliably determine how much institutional
weight a detector result deserves. The resulting problem is not merely technical but procedural:
absent disclosure and corroboration, institutions risk treating a model-dependent signal as though it
were dispositive evidence.
1.2 Related Work and Contribution
Kirchenbauer et al. show that watermark detection can succeed on very short samples under specific
experimental conditions, including relatively strong watermark parameters such as (γ, δ) = (0.25, 2)
in their proof-of-concept setting [1]. That result is important as an engineering demonstration of
detectability, but it does not by itself establish a forensic or regulatory standard for interpreting
detector outputs on short text. Subsequent work has emphasized robustness, distortion-free or
unbiased watermarking, semantic or paraphrase robustness, public detectability, and attack surfaces
such as stealing, spoofing, and scrubbing [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]. Statistical work has also generalized
the efficiency analysis of watermark detectors beyond simple z-tests [8]. Accordingly, this paper
distinguishes three levels of analysis that are often blurred in discussion of AI watermarking:
statistical detectability, practical reliability, and forensic or institutional use. Its contribution is
not a new test statistic. Rather, it uses a simple scaling benchmark to show why low-visibility
watermarking creates an unusually severe short-sample problem for institutional use, and why
the detectability of a signal should not be confused with its reliability for institutional reliance.
That distinction has become more urgent as watermarking and provenance move from research
demonstrations into live governance settings. In the European Union, transparency obligations
under Article 50 of the AI Act become applicable on 2 August 2026,1 and the Commission’s Code
of Practice on Transparency of AI-Generated Content, published on 10 June 2026, now supports
implementation of those Article 50 marking, detection, and labelling obligations on a voluntary
basis. The present framework is not offered as an interpretation of any single legal instrument;
rather, it is offered as a way to discipline evidentiary claims in a policy environment that increasingly
expects marking, detectability, and transparent interpretation to coexist. The interplay among
the EU AI Act’s Article 50 transparency obligations, emerging state-level AI disclosure laws, and
provenance standards such as C2PA increases the practical importance of distinguishing compliance
marks from reliable institutional evidence.2 These legal and technical frameworks variously require,
1See Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Art. 113 (application date); Art. 50 (transparency obligations) [16].
2For example, Article 50 of the EU AI Act addresses marking and detection of AI-generated content; California’s
AI Transparency Act imposes disclosure and detection-tool obligations on covered generative-AI providers for specified
categories of image, video, and audio content [17]; and C2PA provides a technical provenance framework through
2
enable, or begin to standardize machine-readable indications of origin—at different scopes and with
different legal force. But the existence of a mark, manifest, or provenance metadata is not the
same as downstream reliability for institutional reliance. Compliance-oriented outputs should not
be mistaken for decision-ready evidence.3 That observation applies most directly to probabilistic
detection outputs; cryptographic provenance attestations such as C2PA manifests raise distinct
verification questions outside the scope of this paper. What is needed—and what the present
framework is intended to support—is disclosed reliability information showing when a detector
output has been adequately characterized, in terms of error rates, text-length thresholds, and stated
assumptions, for the institutional use to which it is being put.
2 The Statistical Horizon: Formalization
2.1 Key Assumptions
The horizon derivation in this paper rests on three stylized assumptions:
• Approximate token-level independence: Green-list assignments and token draws are
treated as independent for analytic tractability.
• Locally linear watermark response: The increase in green-token probability is proportional
to the logit bias δ for small-to-moderate δ.
• Gaussian approximation: The normalized detector statistic is approximated by a standard
normal distribution under the null and a shifted normal under the alternative.
Each assumption may be violated in practice. Violations (especially positive autocorrelation)
generally increase required sample length, making the stylized derivation optimistic from the
perspective of detectability. The cautionary conclusion is therefore robust in the sense that many
realistic departures from the stylized model do not make undisclosed short-sample attribution
safer. A rough sense of magnitude is useful. Under positive autocorrelation with lag-1 coefficient ρ,
the variance of the green-token count exceeds the independence benchmark by an inflation factor
that, for an AR(1)-like structure, is approximately (1 + ρ)/(1 − ρ). Because the horizon scales
inversely with the variance-normalized signal, the required sample length grows by roughly the
same factor: a modest ρ = 0.3 implies an inflation of about 1.9×, and ρ = 0.5 implies roughly
3×. Token-level dependence is material because green-list assignment is itself seeded by preceding
tokens, so corrections in this range are plausible rather than extreme. The independence-based
horizon should therefore be read as a lower bound on required length, potentially by a factor of
two or more at the text lengths institutions typically evaluate. This estimate is heuristic: the exact
variance structure induced by green-list seeding and decoding is more complex than a simple binary
AR(1), and the figures above are intended as order-of-magnitude intuition rather than calibrated
corrections.
