What this result does not mean. The Statistical Horizon nh is not a universal admissibility
threshold, a minimum token rule applicable across all watermarking systems, or a quantitative
claim about any specific deployed detector. It is a structural scaling result derived under three
stylized assumptions—approximate token independence, locally linear watermark response, and
Gaussian approximation—that are stated precisely because they are known to be optimistic. The
horizon marks the sample length at which expected detector signal reaches threshold under those
assumptions; it does not mark the length at which a detector output becomes suitable for institutional
reliance. That determination requires additional inputs: disclosed system parameters, empirically
characterized error rates, robustness information, and a power analysis appropriate to the decision
being made. The numerical examples in Table 2 are analytic sensitivity illustrations under the
reduced-form model; they are not calibrated claims about real-world detector performance. The
scaling relation above is derived only under the formal definition of η. In the main text, references to
“effective detector response” (ηe) are heuristic and intended for sensitivity reasoning under non-ideal
conditions rather than exact cross-system calculation. The operational value of nh and nE depends
on whether η, or a deployment-specific effective analogue, can be disclosed, estimated, or bounded.
The parameter η is not generally observable from a detector output alone; it depends on the
watermark design, detector construction, and the model’s token-level response to the watermarking
intervention. Where a provider does not disclose enough information to estimate or bound that
response, the horizon cannot be externally computed. In that setting, the framework functions
not as a usable threshold but as a disclosure requirement: the institution lacks the information
needed to determine whether the detector output is reliable at the relevant text length. Even
when treated illustratively, the sensitivity of nh to the combined signal is substantial because
nh ∝ 1/(η
