detectability floor and satisfying a strong standard for institutional reliance are related but distinct
events. The former concerns model-based signal detectability under specified assumptions. The
latter concerns whether the resulting signal is sufficiently reliable, transparent, and interpretable to
carry substantial institutional weight. That second judgment depends not only on the existence
of a signal, but also on desired power, disclosure, calibration, robustness, and the availability of
corroboration.
