showing in an academic-integrity setting might include disclosure of the detector family used,
calibration results on texts of comparable length, reported false-positive behavior under ordinary
editing or paraphrasing, and corroborating non-detector evidence tied to the same submission.
Watermark evidence should not be treated as existing in isolation from other possible attribution
signals. A downstream institution may also consider stylometric indicators, linguistic structure,
document organization, edit history, provenance metadata, or author-specific baselines where
available. But those signals may not be independent. Many of these features may share a common
source in the generative model’s underlying probability distribution, decoding process, or postgeneration editing history, making their apparent independence an assumption rather than a
demonstrated property. Structural correlations are plausible at multiple levels of the text and should
not be assumed absent empirical demonstration: word choice, sentence construction, paragraph
organization, document structure, argument style, and broader writing style. Treating each indicator
as a separate and independent source of evidence can therefore overstate the strength of the overall
attribution.4 The Statistical Horizon problem is one instance of a broader caution: assumptions
about independence, error structure, and evidentiary strength must be examined rather than silently
inherited. In high-stakes settings, an unexamined assumption can become the source of an incorrect
conclusion.
3.3 Base Rates and Multiple Testing
The horizon analysis so far concerns a single text evaluated in isolation. Institutional deployment
rarely looks like that, and two population-level effects further constrain the institutional weight of a
positive detection. First, base rates govern positive predictive value. Even a well-calibrated detector
with a low false-positive rate α will, when applied to a large corpus that is mostly human-written,
return a substantial number of false positives in absolute terms. The probability that a flagged text
is in fact watermarked depends not only on α and detector power but on the prior prevalence of
watermarked text in the population screened. Where that prevalence is low or unknown, a positive
detection on a single short sample carries correspondingly weak posterior support, regardless of how
far the observed statistic exceeds z
∗
. This is the familiar base-rate problem of forensic inference,
and it compounds the short-sample fragility described above rather than substituting for it. Second,
multiple testing inflates expected false positives. An institution screening thousands of submissions
4This is a structural inference rather than a claim about any specific detector or feature set. The empirical
correlation structure among stylometric, organizational, provenance-based, and model-based indicators in AI-generated
text has not been systematically characterized. Conditional independence should therefore be treated as a hypothesis
requiring verification before multiple signals are combined as independent evidence.
7
performs thousands of simultaneous tests; a per-test threshold that appears stringent in isolation
can produce many spurious flags across the corpus. Responsible institutional use therefore requires
error control appropriate to the screening scale—for example, family-wise or false-discovery-rate
adjustment—before any individual flag is treated as meaningful. Neither base-rate sensitivity nor
multiple-testing correction is captured by nh or nE, which describe the detectability and power of a
single test. They are additional constraints on responsible institutional reliance, not consequences
of the horizon itself. The higher-power reliability benchmark nE is a power-analysis benchmark,
not a posterior probability of AI generation or watermark presence. It indicates whether a detector
has enough sample-length-supported signal to achieve a stated power level under the assumed
model. Institutional decisions may also require base-rate information, likelihood ratios, alternative
explanations, and assessment of asymmetric costs. Thus nE can inform institutional reliance, but
it does not by itself complete the Bayesian or decision-theoretic analysis. Even when a detector
output meets or exceeds nE under full disclosure and robustness conditions, institutional weight
still requires base-rate adjustment, multiple-testing control where applicable, corroboration, and
case-specific assessment of asymmetric costs; nE remains an input to that judgment, not a substitute
for it.
3.4 Adversarial Degradation
A further complication is adversarial degradation. In contemporary settings, the threat is not
limited to benign paraphrasing or ordinary editing. Spoofing, watermark stealing or forgery, model
adaptation, and cross-system attack strategies may either weaken a genuine signal or create a
misleading appearance of one [7, 11, 12]. The structural intuition is that reductions in effective
signal-to-noise ratio compound as a sample-length burden, while forged or counterfeited signals
create a distinct false-positive risk. Both attack modes strengthen the case for cautious treatment
unless robustness conditions are disclosed and empirically supported. The cited results give a
qualitative sense of magnitude. Watermark stealing and spoofing attacks can reconstruct enough of
the green-list structure to forge or scrub signals, which in the framework’s terms drives effective ηδ
toward zero for scrubbing and creates false-positive signal where none was generated for spoofing
[7, 11]. Adaptive stealing approaches reduce the text required to approximate the watermark rule,
eroding the very disclosure-and-calibration assumptions on which reliable detection depends [12].
Analyses of production-oriented systems indicate that robustness varies substantially with editing
and paraphrase intensity. The common structural point is that adversarial pressure does not merely
add noise; it can shift the effective operating point of the detector in ways that are not observable
from the detector output alone, which is precisely the condition under which the framework counsels
against strong institutional reliance.
3.5 Practical Summary
• A watermark “hit” on a short passage does not by itself establish origin.
• Reaching the detectability floor nh does not by itself justify strong institutional weight.
• Where system parameters and robustness conditions are undisclosed, the default institutional
posture should be cautious and ordinarily non-attributive.
• Stronger institutional use should require transparent calibration evidence, disclosure of relevant
operating and robustness conditions, and corroboration from independent sources.
8
4 From Detection to Adjudication: A Four-Step Institutional
Sequence
Watermark detection does not, by itself, determine the institutional status of an artifact. Even where
a detector output supports an inference of synthetic involvement at a stated detection threshold and
power level, institutions must still characterize the form and degree of human action surrounding the
artifact’s production, revision, selection, verification, and use. A probabilistic indication of synthetic
origin may be relevant to institutional consequence, but it is not enough by itself. This paper
accordingly distinguishes detection from adjudication through a four-step sequence organized in two
phases. The sequence is schematic rather than jurisdiction-specific: different legal and policy regimes
may attach different consequences to the same forensic signal. The same signal may do different
institutional work: in court, it may bear on reliability; in regulation, it may trigger transparency
obligations. The technical phase comprises the paper’s quantitative contribution: (1) detection
of possible synthetic involvement, and (2) statistical signal and power analysis relative to nh and
nE; it does not itself supply a legal or institutional decision rule. This phase asks whether the
detector output supplies enough model-based signal, under stated assumptions, to justify considering
institutional inquiry. Where a detector cannot satisfy these threshold requirements under the
conditions of use, the sequence provides no basis for institutional escalation. It does not, and
cannot, establish the institutionally relevant questions of human responsibility: what human action
occurred, by whom, under what standard, and with what consequence. The institutional phase
comprises the work that must follow: (3) characterization of post-generative human action, meaning
the professional, legal, or scholarly judgment that occurred after generation, and (4) analysis of
institutional consequence, which depends on the specific duties and standards governing the relevant
domain. Step 3 marks the point at which this paper’s statistical analysis ends and institution-specific
judgment begins. The relevant rubrics are domain-specific and lie outside mathematical verification.
This paper does not propose a universal standard for characterizing post-generative human action;
it identifies the question institutions must answer before a probabilistic signal is converted into
consequence. This sequence is not a prediction that institutions will reliably preserve the distinction;
it identifies the procedural boundary they must preserve if probabilistic detection is to support
legitimate institutional consequence. Consider, as one concrete illustration from legal practice,
an AI-assisted legal brief. The detector output may satisfy the paper’s higher-power benchmark
nE under the assumed model. The relevant institutional question, however, is not whether AI
participated in generation. It is whether the attorney verified the citations and legal propositions,
exercised independent professional judgment, and satisfied applicable procedural and professional
duties, including Rule 11 certification, competence, candor, and supervision. A detector may support
an inference that AI systems contributed to the artifact’s production; it cannot, alone, establish
whether the attorney’s post-generative action met or failed those existing duties. The institutional
consequence of detection therefore depends not on the technical finding in isolation, but on the
application of established professional and procedural standards to the artifact’s full production
history. Characterizing post-generative human action requires evidence categories that institutions
can actually demand and examine. Depending on the setting, these may include prompt logs
showing what was requested of the system; timestamped edit history with authorship attribution;
version diffs documenting how the artifact changed after generation; human verification traces,
such as records of citation-checking or fact-checking; and contemporaneous statements of the role
the author understood the tool to play. None of these is dispositive alone, and their availability
varies by domain. But specifying the categories converts Step 3 from a procedural gesture into a
checklist an institution can be held to, which is the point: the boundary between detection and
9
adjudication is only meaningful if the adjudication side has identifiable inputs. This distinction
reinforces the broader claim of this paper: detection is not adjudication. Provenance signals may
contribute evidentiary context, but they cannot independently resolve authorship, professional
responsibility, or institutional consequence. Institutional frameworks that treat synthetic detection
as self-interpreting risk collapsing materially distinct categories of post-generative human action
into a single procedural label. Synthetic origin may answer how an artifact was produced; it does
not determine what the artifact is evidence of, nor what institutional response detection licenses.
5 Limitations and Scope
This paper is a structural and procedural argument, not a technical or empirical contribution,
and several limitations follow directly from that scope. First, the framework introduces no new
detector, test statistic, or empirical evaluation. The scaling relation is a consequence of a standard
signal-detection model, and the numerical values in Table 2 are analytic illustrations rather than
measurements calibrated to any deployed system. Applying the framework to a specific watermarking
implementation would require estimating η and δ for that system, which this paper does not attempt.
Second, the derivation rests on stylized assumptions: token-level independence, locally linear
watermark response, and a Gaussian approximation. Realistic departures, especially positive
autocorrelation and non-stationarity, generally increase the required sample length, so the formulas
are optimistic from the standpoint of detectability. The paper establishes the direction of this
correction but not its magnitude; quantifying it under realistic mixing conditions is left to future
work. Third, nh and nE are statistical quantities describing the detectability and power of a single
test. They do not encode prior odds, the asymmetric costs of false positives and false negatives,
robustness to model misspecification, or adversarial tampering. As Section 3 emphasizes, base
rates, multiple-testing control, disclosure, and corroboration are additional inputs or constraints for
institutional reliance; the benchmarks are inputs to that judgment, not substitutes for it. Fourth, the
legal and policy discussion is deliberately schematic. The four-step sequence identifies a procedural
boundary; it does not map the proposed benchmarks onto specific standards of proof (for example,
preponderance of the evidence versus beyond a reasonable doubt), nor does it work through a
particular adjudicated case. Those mappings are domain-specific and lie outside the statistical scope
of this paper. This paper avoids using “sufficiency” as a label for its proposed statistical benchmarks.
In evidence law, “sufficiency,” “conditional relevance,” “admissibility,” and “expert reliability”
have specific meanings under rules such as Rule 104(b), Rule 702, Daubert, and related state-law
standards: Rule 104(b) concerns whether evidence is sufficient to support a finding of a conditional
fact, and the 2023 amendment to Rule 702 requires the proponent to establish, more likely than not,
that expert testimony satisfies each of the rule’s requirements. The Statistical Horizon framework
instead uses the language of detectability, reliability, power, and institutional reliance. Its proposed
benchmarks ask whether a detector output has enough sample-length-supported signal to inform
a proposed attribution inference under stated assumptions; they do not determine admissibility,
legal sufficiency, or ultimate institutional responsibility. The paper accordingly does not propose
a substitute for Rule 104(b), Rule 702, Daubert, any state-law analogue such as Frye or hybrid
admissibility standards, or any jurisdiction-specific evidentiary rule, nor does it purport to supply
a governing standard for non-judicial institutional contexts not governed by the Federal Rules
of Evidence. If a watermark detector output were offered through expert testimony in a federal
proceeding, Rule 702 would require the proponent to establish that the testimony rests on sufficient
facts or data, reliable principles and methods, and reliable application to the facts of the case.
As clarified by the 2023 amendment, this is a Rule 104(a) admissibility determination, made by
10
the court on a more-likely-than-not standard, rather than a Rule 104(b) question of conditional
relevance. The disclosures proposed here—text-length reliability curves, stated false-positive and
false-negative rates, robustness assumptions, and limits on use—are technical inputs that may
inform a court’s gatekeeping analysis, but they do not determine admissibility or satisfy Rule 702
on their own.5 The framework likewise does not address Rule 401 relevance, authentication, hearsay,
or other ordinary evidentiary doctrines. A detector output might be minimally relevant in the
Rule 401 sense while still failing Rule 702 reliability, Rule 104(a) gatekeeping, institutional policy
requirements, or a merits standard of proof. These limitations do not undermine the central claim;
they locate it. The contribution is to discipline how a probabilistic signal is interpreted, not to
certify any particular detector or to resolve any particular institutional case.
6 Conclusion: The Structural Limit of Probabilistic Attribution
AI text watermarking represents a meaningful advance in digital provenance. Yet its significance for
institutional reliance is constrained not only by implementation choices, but by structural limits
imposed by statistical inference on finite text samples. This paper formalizes the Statistical Horizon
as a scaling benchmark for that problem and argues that detectability should not be confused with
reliability for institutional reliance. The resulting claim is narrower than a universal admissibility rule,
but more robust. As watermark strength decreases and operating conditions become less transparent,
required sample length rises sharply and interpretation becomes increasingly dependent on hidden
parameters, detector calibration, and disclosure. Under such conditions, short-text detections are
especially poor candidates for strong institutional attribution. The paper’s contribution is not a new
statistical test. It is the use of a simple scaling benchmark to show why low-visibility watermarking
creates an unusually severe short-sample problem for institutional use, and why a detectability
floor may sit well below a plausible reliability benchmark. In that sense, the framework can inform
disclosure and reliance weighting for probabilistic watermark signals, but it does not itself determine
admissibility, proof, or institutional outcomes. In an era of layered provenance and probabilistic
attribution, structural limits are not obstacles to governance. They are conditions for governing
responsibly.
