C Extension Notes on Detector Classes
The formal derivation in Appendix A is specific to a z-test-style mean-shift detector. Other detector
families, including likelihood-ratio or Bayesian detectors, may have different operating constants,
calibration procedures, and robustness profiles. The present paper does not claim that all such
detectors can be reduced exactly to the same one-parameter representation. What the paper claims
is narrower. Across detector families, finite-sample performance plausibly depends on the magnitude
of the induced separation between null and alternative distributions and on the amount of text
available. The policy argument of the main text therefore does not rest on the claim that all
detectors behave identically. It rests on the more modest point that opaque, parameter-sensitive,
finite-sample statistical methods should not be given strong institutional weight without transparent
support.
